---
title: Mapping & Validation
description: Key resolution, nested types, defaults, nullability, and strict conversion rules.
---

## Key resolution

Keys are matched case-insensitively. For each constructor parameter, Yacla tries an explicit `@Key`, the parameter name, snake_case, and kebab-case, in that order. For `apiKey`, the automatic candidates are `apikey`, `api_key`, and `api-key`.

An explicit key is preferred, but automatic candidates are still tried if it is absent. Unknown document keys are not constructor arguments; they can remain in the file.

## Defaults and nulls

| Input | Result |
| --- | --- |
| Missing field with a Kotlin default | Uses the constructor default |
| Missing nullable field without a default | Uses null |
| Missing required non-null field | Throws `YaclaConfigException` |
| Explicit null for a nullable field | Keeps null, even if a default exists |
| Explicit null for a non-null field | Throws, even if a default exists |

`@BlankToNull` and `@SetOf` can produce null from a non-null raw value. In that case a constructor default can still be selected.

## Nested objects and collections

```kotlin
enum class Mode { DEVELOPMENT, PRODUCTION }

data class ServerConfig(val host: String, val port: Int = 8080)

data class AppConfig(
    val servers: List<ServerConfig> = emptyList(),
    val modes: Set<Mode> = emptySet(),
    val limits: Map<String, Int> = emptyMap()
)
```

```yaml
servers:
  - host: localhost
    port: 8080
modes: [development, production]
limits:
  requests: 100
```

List, set, and map elements are converted recursively. Nested classes need primary constructors. Enum matching is case-insensitive, including collection elements; `@EnumList` and `@EnumSet` are optional. Scalar values supplied for a list or set are treated as a single element.

## Conversion failures

Invalid values cause `YaclaConfigException` instead of silently using defaults or dropping elements. Integer conversion rejects fractions and overflow. Float and double conversion rejects non-finite values. Booleans accept `true` and `false` case-insensitively.

Errors identify the constructor field path, for example `servers[0].port`. A default does not rescue a present but invalid value. Correct the source file before reloading.
